<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beit She'an</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Scythopolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beit She'an is a town in northern Israel at the junction of the Jordan and Jezreel valleys. An Egyptian stronghold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, it became the Greek city of Scythopolis, the only member of the Decapolis west of the Jordan, and a major Roman and Byzantine city. The earthquake of 749 destroyed it, and its extensive ruins now form a national park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beit-shean.jpg" alt="Beit She'an" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omer berner · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beit_shean.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
